#sleela 3.0
<< spill-annotation-demo.sleela -- the runnable reference for the syntax 3.0
   @spill loop annotation (SLEELA.syntax section 33).

   @spill is a STATEMENT-LEVEL, BEHAVIORAL annotation that attaches to a
   for/while/do loop. Unlike @loopDelay/@loopBudget, which pause on EVERY
   iteration, @spill "counts as a timer start itself": it marks the loop entry
   as a timer-start checkpoint and pauses ONCE, before the first iteration, so
   the loop can seemingly pause before it starts.

   A @spill may also name up to two subjects -- the judges and the witnesses of
   the loop's performs (the observers of what the loop does). The subjects are
   loop metadata and have no runtime effect.

     @spill                                  bare checkpoint, 0 ms pause
     @spill(<N>)                             pause N ms once before iterating
     @spill(ms = <N>)                        pause N ms once
     @spill(seconds = <N>)                   pause N seconds once
     @spill(judges = <subject>)              name the judge of performs
     @spill(witnesses = <subject>)           name the witness of performs
     @spill(seconds = 1, judges = Panel, witnesses = AuditLog)   all together

   It lowers to OP_SLEEP, emitted ONCE before the loop (not per iteration).

   Run it (it takes a couple of seconds by design):
     sleela run examples/spill-annotation-demo.sleela >>
class SpillAnnotationDemo {

    void main() {
        print("1) for with a bare @spill: a checkpoint, no pause");
        @spill
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i = i + 1) {
            print("   for tick " + i);
        }

        print("2) while with @spill(ms = 300): pause ~300ms ONCE before start");
        int j = 0;
        @spill(ms = 300)
        while (j < 3) {
            print("   while tick " + j);
            j = j + 1;
        }

        print("3) for with named judges and witnesses of performs");
        @spill(ms = 200, judges = ReviewPanel, witnesses = AuditLog)
        for (int k = 0; k < 2; k = k + 1) {
            print("   judged/witnessed tick " + k);
        }

        print("4) @spill (once, up front) combined with @loopDelay (per pass)");
        @spill(seconds = 1)
        @loopDelay(ms = 200)
        for (int m = 0; m < 2; m = m + 1) {
            print("   spilled-then-delayed tick " + m);
        }

        print("done");
    }
}
